Fiverr $FVRR maybe the most hated stock. Down 97% from its peak a few years ago. And down 57% this year. Most importantly has no debt and sitting on cash equal to its market cap. It's basically free. It also generates material GAAP net income and FCF even during revenue freefall.

The bull case is that you're getting a profitable, cash generating business for free while it gets repriced as an AI loser. You are paying literally zero for a business that has stayed profitable through the downturn. Plus there's early green shoots seen in its shift to upmarket.

This isn't financial advice and have a long position in the stock which I've been buying the past couple weeks. In fact i'm buying more today as I post. I do think it has been substantially derisked. Capital return and takeover options are real. Sentiment completely washed out

@hunterorrell I was thinking why didn't they lean heavier into clipping marketplaces. They let Mr. Beast run away with that

The bear case is that Fiverr is a value trap. The cash is real. But the business underneath is shrinking fast enough that the cash ends up funding the decline rather than coming back to shareholders. Consensus is that the problem is structural and not cyclical. What do you think?

I think one thing we've learned is that people are still willing to pay people to do things. Even if it's simple to us to run prompts people are willing to pay for an expert to do it. Services like writing and translation of course are in free fall. Other things could surprisingly inflect to growth.

This is the consensus and certainly data supports this view till now. That's why revenue fell off a cliff. However the $1000+ job category has been growing quite rapidly and could inflect soon. Meanwhile the company is still earning money so it has time to wait. You're essentially buying it for free would be my argument. I don't see too many opportunities to buy something that generates cash flow and profits for literally nothing

The move to answer engines away from search has also heard it in terms of traffic generation so now it's trying to get well positioned within the answer engines. I know that's a big initiative and I see job postings for it. But also a partnership with the major LLM isn't out of the question and would be a major catalyst. Because like you said click here to have a human do it is a great idea.

@hunterorrell Let's say it makes a deal with Anthropic for instance for something like that what is a stock do 50% in a day up? Major catalyst I think and not priced in.

